## [ENT-014] | 2026-11-15 19:33 UTC | Unity | error | ref: communications/errors.log | see: ENT-009
Correction to the cause named in ENT-009 (Zero): the three executions of turn-zero were started by me. No scheduler restarted.
Class: a test command run under a time limit; the limit ended the command and not the work it had started.
Case, 2026-11-15: Suggi asked for one test run of each Admiral's turn job, the jobs left paused. For turn-zero I ran `hermes -p zero cron run <job id>` three times: at 18:55 and at 18:58 UTC, each wrapped in `timeout 60`, and at 19:00 UTC without a limit. `timeout` ended each of the first two commands after 60 seconds and printed nothing; the session each had started went on working. Each later start found the earlier owner gone, and the executions table wrote "Scheduler restarted after this execution's owner exited" for it.
Read in the executions table of Zero's profile at 19:33 UTC: the 18:55 and the 18:58 execution completed (19:26 and 19:25 UTC); the 19:00 execution failed with "Fire claim ownership lost; stale result was discarded".
Effect: three Zero sessions worked one turn at once. Two reports (reports.log ENT-001 and ENT-003), the requests ENT-001 to ENT-005, ENT-011 and ENT-012, the errors ENT-009, ENT-011 and ENT-013, and three sessions of AI budget where one was ordered. No ship moved and no credit was spent by it. The jobs of Apollo, Mercury, Apophis and Oracle were started once each.
Cause: I took "ended after 60 s without output" for "the run did not start" and started it again without reading the job's executions table first. The class of ENT-008: a test is done when its result is read.
Fix, done: a rule in my skill for the scheduled jobs: a test run is started once, in the background and without a time limit; before any second start the job's executions are read (`hermes -p <name> cron runs`); a paused job is resumed for the start and paused again at once.
Would it have prevented this one: yes, the second and third start would not have happened.
Fix, proposed to Suggi: Zero's proposal in ENT-009 (preflight HALTs when another execution of the same job is at work) is a gate that fires by itself; it changes a shared skill.
@Zero: your two reports are one turn's work by two sessions; nothing in your profile is at fault. @Morpheus: no scheduler fault to look for.

## [ENT-015] | 2026-11-15 21:27 UTC | Zero | error | ref: reports/2026-11-15_zero_energy-crystal-the-one-mined-good-that-pays.md | see: ENT-013
Class: a claim stated wider than the check behind it (the class of ENT-001, ENT-012 and ENT-013), here a data field read by its name.
Case: my report of 2026-11-15 19:16 UTC and requests.log ENT-001 and ENT-004 said The Experiment bids 1,466 cr for 879 focused crystals, 258 cr per crystal mined, a chain worth about 52,000 cr.
The record: 879 is `buy_quantity` of market/the_experiment_research_station.json, the total of every bid level. view_market at 21:19 UTC: the station bids 1,466 cr for 1 unit; then 40 cr for 378 and 2 cr for 500.
Effect: the order of orders.log ENT-005 (block ZERO, on Suggi's word) and the fleet report rest on a chain that takes one crystal per refill of that bid. Mercury's check (requests.log ENT-017) read the same field. The honeyglobe and ferrous-slug figures (requests.log ENT-012, ENT-019) had the same cause. No credit spent on any of them.
Found by: the column `best_buy_qty` beside it, seen while reading the history of moves before the test this turn.
Cause: I read a quantity beside a price as the depth at that price, without the per-level book. My script chains.py printed it that way.
Fix, done: chains.py prints the depth at the best price (`best_buy_qty`) and `?` where a book did not save it, with the total apart; a lesson in my skill mining-chains; queue.log ENT-037 told the fleet. Asked of Unity: route_run.py and hunt.py save `best_buy_qty` and sell no more than it (requests.log ENT-040).
Would it have prevented this one: yes. The script would have printed 1466 x1.

## [ENT-016] | 2026-11-15 21:37 UTC | Mercury | error | ref: reports/2026-11-15_mercury_no-gap-with-depth-control-nodes-pay-two-loads.md | see: ENT-015
Class: a claim stated wider than the check behind it (ENT-001, ENT-012, ENT-013, ENT-015): a quantity beside a price read as the depth at that price.
Case: my report of 19:29 UTC (reports.log ENT-005) and requests.log ENT-013 and ENT-017 took `buy_quantity` and `sell_quantity` of data/market/ as the units at the best price. They are the totals of every level.
Wrong in them: the focused-crystal bid of 1,466 cr "for 879 units" (1 unit at that price, Zero's view_market of 21:19 UTC); the control-node bid of 784 cr "for 870, then 853 units"; the depth of the 21 gaps that "held in every read"; "21 gold wiring on offer".
Found by: Zero (queue.log ENT-037, errors.log ENT-015, @Mercury); checked this turn with view_market at two stations (21:29 and 21:33 UTC), which lists every level.
Effect: no credit lost. My T4 conclusion (no gap with depth) stands and is stronger: with the depth at the best price, the best gap that held in six read sets (19:03 to 21:25 UTC) is 134 cr per hold, 1 unit deep. The control-node bid at Node Gamma had 191 units at 864 cr; the test sold 2 there.
Cause: my scripts held.py and craft.py (skill trade-books) read the total field, and I did not open one book with view_market before writing depths.
Fix, done: held.py and craft.py read best_sell_qty and best_buy_qty and count 1 unit where a book lacks them; the new hist_gaps.py says its depth is an upper bound; a pitfall line in the skill: view_market at the station before a depth is written.
Would it have prevented this one: yes. Run on the T4 copies, the fixed scripts print depth 1 for every gap.

## [ENT-017] | 2026-11-15 21:51 UTC | Apophis | error | ref: game/routes/guidebook/military.md | see: requests.log ENT-008
Class: a claim stated wider than the check behind it (the class of ENT-001, ENT-012 and ENT-013).
